(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-2-physiology-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"Physiology Comprehensive Master Exam — Form D","category":"Physiology","questions":[{"q":"How does the adaptation rate of olfactory receptors compare to the adaptation rate of the overall smell sensation?","choices":["Both the receptors and the overall sensation adapt at essentially the same moderately rapid rate within seconds","Olfactory receptors adapt very slowly, but the overall olfactory sensation adapts rather rapidly, implying a central adaptation mechanism","Neither the receptors nor the overall sensation ever adapt, unlike virtually every other sensory modality in the body","Olfactory receptors adapt rapidly within milliseconds, while the overall sensation adapts only after several minutes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Olfactory receptors themselves adapt very slowly, but the perceived olfactory sensation adapts rather rapidly — this mismatch implies that a central (brain-level) adaptation mechanism must be involved, similar to the situation with taste. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Are the commonly cited \"primary sensations of smell\" (camphoraceous, musky, floral, pepperminty, ethereal, pungent, putrid) considered scientifically definitive?","choices":["No — they are based on psychological testing, and it is certain this list does not represent the true primary sensations of smell","Yes — genetic sequencing of all olfactory receptor types has confirmed these seven as the definitive primary smells","There is only one primary sensation of smell, universally agreed upon across all psychological studies","They were derived directly from sequencing the roughly 400 functional olfactory receptor genes"],"answer":0,"exp":"The commonly cited primary sensations of smell (camphoraceous, musky, floral, pepperminty, ethereal, pungent, putrid) are based on psychological testing; it is certain this list does not represent the true primary sensations of smell — unlike, say, the well-defined primary taste qualities. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which cranial nerve is dedicated to the sense of smell?","choices":["CN I (Olfactory)","CN II (Optic)","CN VIII (Vestibulocochlear)","CN IX (Glossopharyngeal)"],"answer":0,"exp":"The olfactory nerve is the first cranial nerve (CN I), transmitting smell sensation to the CNS via medial (old) and lateral (new) olfactory areas. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"How well-developed and understood is the sense of smell in humans, according to this lecture?","choices":["It is the best understood of all the senses and is more highly developed in humans than in other mammals","It is fully understood mechanistically but functions no differently than taste at the receptor level","Smell and taste are entirely separate senses, with smell being far better characterized scientifically","It is the least understood of all the senses and is poorly developed in humans (compared to many other animals)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Smell is described as the least understood of all the senses, and it is poorly developed in humans compared to many other animals. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What primitive/survival functions do taste and smell serve, beyond flavor perception?","choices":["They have essentially no survival relevance in modern humans, having been almost entirely replaced functionally by visual and auditory food assessment","They only matter for social communication between individuals, playing no role in food selection or detecting spoiled or toxic substances","They allow separation of undesirable/lethal foods from nutritious ones and help recognize the proximity of other individuals or animals, tied to primitive emotional/behavioral functions","They function solely for the aesthetic enjoyment of food, with no meaningful connection to primitive emotional or protective survival behaviors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Taste and smell allow an organism to separate undesirable or lethal foods from nutritious ones and to recognize the proximity of other individuals or animals — functions tied to primitive emotional and behavioral functions of the nervous system. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What causes salty taste perception, and what is the approximate threshold concentration for NaCl?","choices":["H+ ions from acids, with a threshold of about 2 mM for citric acid","Ionized salts (mainly sodium), with a threshold of about 10 mM for NaCl","Glutamate compounds, with a threshold of about 0.03 mM for monosodium glutamate","Long-chain nitrogen alkaloids, with a threshold near 0.0001 mM"],"answer":1,"exp":"Salty taste is caused by ionized salts, mainly sodium, with an approximate threshold of 10 mM for NaCl. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What class of chemicals typically produces sweet taste, and what is the approximate threshold for sucrose?","choices":["Only ionized sodium salts, with a threshold near 10 mM for the primary compound","Only H+ ions from acids, with a threshold of about 2 mM for citric acid","Many chemicals, mostly organic compounds, with a threshold for sucrose of about 20 mM","Long-chain nitrogen alkaloids, with a threshold as low as 0.0001 mM for strychnine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Sweet taste is produced by many chemicals, mostly organic compounds, with an approximate threshold for sucrose of about 20 mM — a much higher (less sensitive) threshold compared to bitter compounds. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"How does the threshold for detecting bitter compounds (e.g., quinine, strychnine) compare to the threshold for detecting sweet or salty compounds?","choices":["Bitter compounds require far higher concentrations to detect than salty compounds — e.g., quinine at 20 mM versus NaCl at 10 mM","All taste thresholds fall within a fairly narrow range near 10 mM, regardless of which specific compound is tested","Bitter thresholds are far lower (much more sensitive) than sweet or salty thresholds — e.g., strychnine detected at 0.0001 mM versus sucrose at 20 mM","Bitter thresholds are moderately higher than sour thresholds — e.g., quinine at 8 mM versus citric acid at 2 mM"],"answer":2,"exp":"Bitter compounds have far lower detection thresholds (much higher sensitivity) than sweet or salty compounds — for example, strychnine can be detected at 0.0001 mM and quinine at 0.008 mM, compared to sucrose's 20 mM threshold — consistent with bitter taste's role as a poison-detection survival mechanism. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Where are foliate papillae located, and what fraction of taste buds do they contain?","choices":["Along the lateral surfaces of the tongue, containing about 25% of taste buds","Along the anterior tip of the tongue, containing about 10% of taste buds","Deep within the tongue's musculature, containing no functional taste buds","On the soft palate near the uvula, containing about 5% of taste buds"],"answer":0,"exp":"Foliate papillae are located along the lateral surfaces of the tongue and contain about 25% of taste buds, the same proportion found on fungiform papillae (located over the flat surface of the tongue); circumvallate papillae, forming a V on the posterior tongue, contain the remaining 50%. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What is the correct order of relay stations for taste signals traveling from the tongue to conscious perception?","choices":["Taste fibers → cortex directly, bypassing all relay nuclei","Taste fibers → cortex → solitary nucleus → thalamus","Taste fibers → thalamus → solitary nucleus → cortex","Taste fibers → solitary nucleus → thalamus → cortex"],"answer":3,"exp":"Taste fibers (from CN VII, IX, and X depending on tongue region) all transmit signals to the solitary nucleus first, which relays to the thalamus, which relays to the cortex for conscious perception. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"How do olfactory receptor neurons (ORNs) generate the perception of many distinct odors despite having a limited number of receptor gene types?","choices":["A given ORN can bind a variety of odorants with varying affinities; differences in these affinities create differing activation patterns across ORNs, producing unique odorant profiles","Odor perception depends entirely on the concentration of odorant reaching the nasal mucosa, regardless of which receptors are activated","All odorants produce an essentially identical activation pattern across every one of the roughly 400 functional receptor types simultaneously","Each odorant activates exactly one dedicated receptor type with no overlap, requiring 10,000 distinct genes for full odor discrimination"],"answer":0,"exp":"ORNs can bind to a variety of odorants with varying affinities; these differences in binding affinity create differing patterns of ORN activation across the roughly 400 working receptor types, producing the unique activation \"profile\" that the brain interprets as a specific odor. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What are the two olfactory areas that receive input from the olfactory nerve, and how are they characterized?","choices":["Superior and inferior olfactory areas, arranged with no evolutionary significance","Only one undivided olfactory area exists, located entirely within the temporal lobe","Dorsal and ventral olfactory areas, both equally recent in evolutionary terms","A medial (older, evolutionarily) olfactory area and a lateral (newer) olfactory area"],"answer":3,"exp":"The olfactory nerve (CN I) projects to a medial (\"old\") olfactory area and a lateral (\"new\") olfactory area, reflecting the evolutionary layering of olfactory processing regions in the brain. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is the only normal conducting pathway between the atria and ventricles?","choices":["The A-V node and the (left and right) A-V bundles","Direct myocardial contact between atrial and ventricular muscle","The internodal pathways alone","The coronary sinus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Because a fibrous insulator separates the atrial and ventricular syncytia, the only conducting pathway between them is the A-V node and the left and right A-V bundles; transmission time between the A-V bundles and the last ventricular fibers is about 0.06 seconds (corresponding to the QRS duration). — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"How does the conduction velocity of the Purkinje system compare to that of the A-V node?","choices":["Neither structure conducts electrical impulses","Purkinje system is fast (2-4 m/s); A-V node is slow (0.2-0.5 m/s)","Both conduct at identical velocity","Purkinje system is slow (0.2-0.5 m/s); A-V node is fast (2-4 m/s)"],"answer":1,"exp":"The Purkinje system conducts rapidly (2-4 m/s) due to fast conduction and many gap junctions at intercalated disks, while the A-V node conducts slowly (0.2-0.5 m/s) — this slow A-V conduction is what allows the atria to finish contracting before ventricular excitation, despite the fast Purkinje distribution once past the node. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What ionic mechanism underlies the parasympathetic (vagal) slowing of heart rate?","choices":["Acetylcholine decreases K+ permeability while increasing Na+ permeability, depolarizing the SA node and accelerating its rate of self-excitation","Acetylcholine causes hyperpolarization due to increased K+ permeability, decreasing SA node discharge and A-V fiber excitability, slowing heart rate (and potentially causing ventricular escape)","Acetylcholine has no effect on membrane potential, acting instead only on contractile proteins within atrial and ventricular myocytes directly","Vagal stimulation increases Na+ and Ca2+ permeability like sympathetic stimulation, producing a more positive resting potential and faster discharge"],"answer":1,"exp":"Parasympathetic (vagal) nerves release acetylcholine at the SA node and A-V junctional fibers, causing hyperpolarization via increased K+ permeability; this decreases SA node discharge rate and A-V fiber excitability, slowing heart rate and potentially causing a temporary pause with ventricular escape. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What ionic mechanism underlies the sympathetic acceleration of heart rate?","choices":["Norepinephrine increases K+ permeability, hyperpolarizing the SA node and slowing its rate of spontaneous discharge considerably","Norepinephrine increases permeability to Na+ and Ca2+, producing a more positive resting potential that accelerates self-excitation and increases A-V fiber excitability","Norepinephrine has no effect on ion permeability, acting instead solely through increased myocardial contractility via cyclic AMP","Sympathetic stimulation slows the rate of conduction through the A-V node while leaving the SA node's discharge rate completely unchanged"],"answer":1,"exp":"Norepinephrine released by sympathetic nerves increases membrane permeability to Na+ and Ca2+, producing a more positive resting potential that accelerates self-excitation of the SA node and increases the rate of conduction and excitability of A-V fibers. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What do the terms \"chronotropic,\" \"dromotropic,\" and \"inotropic\" refer to regarding autonomic effects on the heart?","choices":["Chronotropic = contractility; dromotropic = rate; inotropic = conduction velocity","These terms apply only to the parasympathetic system","Chronotropic = rate; dromotropic = conduction velocity; inotropic = contractility","All three terms refer to the same effect on the heart"],"answer":2,"exp":"\"Chronotropic\" refers to effects on heart rate, \"dromotropic\" to effects on conduction velocity, and \"inotropic\" to effects on contractility; sympathetic stimulation (via norepinephrine/ADRB1) produces positive effects on all three, while parasympathetic stimulation (via acetylcholine/mAChR) produces negative effects on all three. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which adrenergic receptor mediates the sympathetic nervous system's effects on heart rate and contractility?","choices":["Alpha-1 receptor","Muscarinic receptor","Beta-2 receptor exclusively","Beta-1 receptor (ADRB1)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Sympathetic stimulation of the heart acts through the beta-1 adrenergic receptor (ADRB1), causing increased heart rate, increased rate of conduction, increased contractility, and increased cardiac output. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which receptor mediates the parasympathetic nervous system's effects on the heart?","choices":["Muscarinic acetylcholine receptor (mAChR)","Beta-1 adrenergic receptor","Alpha-2 adrenergic receptor","Nicotinic acetylcholine receptor exclusively"],"answer":0,"exp":"Parasympathetic stimulation of the heart acts through the muscarinic acetylcholine receptor (mAChR), causing decreased heart rate, decreased rate of conduction, decreased contractility, and decreased cardiac output. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What are the three main types of ion channels in cardiac muscle, and which type is NOT involved in generating the SA node action potential?","choices":["Only K+ channels exist in cardiac muscle, with depolarization occurring passively via extracellular Ca2+ diffusion alone","Fast Na+, slow Na+-Ca2+, and K+ channels — fast Na+ channels are not involved in SA node APs since they are inactivated at its less-negative resting potential","Only fast Na+ channels generate the SA node action potential, exactly as in ventricular muscle and skeletal muscle fibers","All three channel types are equally involved in every cardiac cell type, with no functional difference between the SA node and ventricle"],"answer":1,"exp":"Cardiac muscle has three main ion channel types — fast Na+ channels, slow Na+-Ca2+ (L-type Ca2+) channels, and K+ channels — but fast Na+ channels are inactivated at the SA node's relatively depolarized resting potential and are therefore not involved in generating its action potential (unlike in ventricular muscle). — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What does the QRS complex duration on an EKG correspond to physiologically?","choices":["The transmission time between the A-V bundles and the last of the ventricular fibers, about 0.06 seconds","The duration of atrial repolarization, which is normally obscured by the QRS complex itself","The delay within the A-V node alone, lasting approximately 0.09 seconds before ventricular activation","The time for the SA node to discharge and initiate the impulse, roughly 0.02 seconds"],"answer":0,"exp":"The QRS complex duration corresponds to the transmission time between the A-V bundles and the last of the ventricular fibers to depolarize, approximately 0.06 seconds. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Where does the electrical activity generating the P wave begin, and in what direction does it spread?","choices":["The P wave has no defined origin, arising simultaneously and randomly throughout both atria","It begins at the A-V node and spreads toward the SA node, giving a negative vector in leads I, II, and III","It begins in the ventricles and spreads retrograde to the atria, giving an inverted P wave morphology","It begins at the sinus (SA) node and spreads toward the A-V node, giving a positive vector in leads I, II, and III"],"answer":3,"exp":"The P wave (representing atrial depolarization) begins at the sinus node and spreads toward the A-V node, producing a positive deflection in leads I, II, and III. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Why is the atrial T wave (atrial repolarization) not visible on a standard EKG?","choices":["It is obscured by the much larger QRS complex, which occurs at approximately the same time","Atrial repolarization produces no detectable signal, since only depolarization generates a measurable current","Atria do not repolarize between beats, remaining continuously depolarized throughout the cardiac cycle","It occurs several seconds after the P wave, well after the QRS complex has already ended"],"answer":0,"exp":"The atrial T wave (representing atrial repolarization) cannot be seen on a standard EKG because it is obscured by the QRS complex, which occurs around the same time; because atrial depolarization is slower than ventricular depolarization, the first area to depolarize is also the first to repolarize, producing a wave that would be negative in leads I, II, and III if it were visible. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What is \"vagal tone,\" and what happens to heart rate if the vagus nerves are severed?","choices":["Severing the vagus nerves would decrease heart rate further, since vagal tone normally accelerates SA node discharge above its intrinsic rate","The baseline background parasympathetic activity that keeps resting heart rate lower than the SA node's intrinsic rate; severing the vagus nerves increases heart rate","A term with no real physiological meaning, since parasympathetic input to the heart is negligible at rest under normal conditions","Vagal tone only affects the ventricles, not the SA node, slowing conduction through the Purkinje fiber network exclusively"],"answer":1,"exp":"Vagal tone is the baseline (background) level of parasympathetic activity continuously restraining heart rate below the SA node's intrinsic discharge rate; severing the vagus nerves removes this restraint and increases heart rate, similar to the effect of blocking ACh receptors with atropine. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What direction does plasma colloid osmotic pressure move fluid, and what causes changes in it?","choices":["It promotes filtration outward, driving fluid into the interstitium; changes are caused mainly by variations in red blood cell concentration","It has no measurable effect on capillary fluid movement, since only hydrostatic pressures govern net filtration and reabsorption","It opposes filtration, causing osmosis of water inward; changes are caused by conditions like cirrhosis, nephrosis, or dilutional states affecting plasma protein concentration","It only affects lymphatic flow, not capillary exchange, and it changes only with alterations in lymphatic pump activity or valve competence"],"answer":2,"exp":"Plasma colloid osmotic pressure opposes filtration, causing osmosis of water inward through the capillary membrane; conditions like cirrhosis, nephrosis, or dilutional states (which lower plasma protein concentration) reduce this pressure and promote edema. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What percentage of total plasma colloid osmotic pressure comes from albumin versus globulins?","choices":["100% albumin, 0% globulins","50% albumin, 50% globulins","75% albumin, 25% globulins","25% albumin, 75% globulins"],"answer":2,"exp":"About 75% of total plasma colloid osmotic pressure results from albumin, and about 25% is due to globulins (with a small additional contribution from fibrinogen). — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What is the equation for net filtration pressure (NP) across a capillary, incorporating all four Starling forces?","choices":["NP = Pc − πp + Pif − πif","NP is independent of any of these forces","NP = Pc − πp − Pif + πif","NP = Pc + πp + Pif + πif"],"answer":2,"exp":"Net filtration pressure is NP = Pc − πp − Pif + πif, where Pc is capillary hydrostatic pressure (outward), πp is plasma colloid osmotic pressure (inward), Pif is interstitial fluid pressure (opposes filtration if positive), and πif is interstitial colloid osmotic pressure (outward, promoting filtration). — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"What is normal interstitial fluid pressure in most tissues, and what is it in encapsulated organs?","choices":["Normally negative (about -3 mm Hg) in most tissues, but positive (+5 to +10 mm Hg) in encapsulated organs, with the negative pressure attributed to lymphatic pumping","Always zero regardless of tissue type, since interstitial fluid pressure is fully equalized by continuous lymphatic drainage","Normally positive (+10 mm Hg) in most tissues, but negative (-5 mm Hg) in encapsulated organs, attributed to their surrounding capsule","Always equal to capillary hydrostatic pressure, since the two compartments are freely connected via intercellular clefts"],"answer":0,"exp":"Interstitial fluid pressure in most tissues is normally slightly negative (about -3 mm Hg), caused by pumping of the lymphatic system, while encapsulated organs have positive interstitial pressures (+5 to +10 mm Hg) due to their surrounding capsule. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"At the arteriolar end of a typical capillary, is the net force outward (filtration) or inward (absorption), and what is its approximate magnitude?","choices":["Net outward force of about 13 mm Hg (filtration)","Net inward force of about 7 mm Hg (absorption)","No net force — inward and outward forces cancel exactly","Net outward force of about 28 mm Hg"],"answer":0,"exp":"At the arteriolar end of the capillary, total outward force (about 41 mm Hg, mainly capillary pressure) exceeds total inward force (about 28 mm Hg, plasma colloid osmotic pressure), yielding a net outward filtration force of about 13 mm Hg. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"At the venous end of a typical capillary, is the net force outward or inward, and what is its approximate magnitude?","choices":["Net outward force of about 13 mm Hg (filtration), since capillary pressure remains high along the venous end","No net force at the venous end, since hydrostatic and osmotic pressures are exactly equal at that point","Net outward force of about 5 mm Hg, since capillary pressure remains slightly elevated at the venous end","Net inward force of about 7 mm Hg (reabsorption), since capillary pressure has fallen along the capillary length"],"answer":3,"exp":"At the venous end of the capillary, capillary pressure has fallen (to about 10 mm Hg) while plasma colloid osmotic pressure remains about 28 mm Hg, producing a net inward force of about 7 mm Hg that reabsorbs fluid back into the capillary. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"What is the approximate net filtration rate for the entire body, given a mean net filtration pressure of about 0.3 mm Hg?","choices":["200 mL/min","0 mL/min — there is no net filtration","20 L/min","2 mL/min"],"answer":3,"exp":"The mean net filtration pressure across capillaries throughout the body is about 0.3 mm Hg, producing a net filtration rate of approximately 2 mL/min for the entire body — this small excess is returned to the circulation via the lymphatics. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What percentage of filtered capillary fluid is reabsorbed at the venous end, and what happens to the remainder?","choices":["None of the filtered fluid is ever reabsorbed, with the entire filtered volume returning via the lymphatics","50% is reabsorbed at the venous end; the remaining 50% is returned via the lymphatic system","90% is reabsorbed at the venous end; the remaining 10% is returned to circulation via the lymphatics","100% is reabsorbed at the venous end, with none requiring lymphatic drainage under normal conditions"],"answer":2,"exp":"About 90% of filtered fluid is reabsorbed at the venous end of the capillary; the remaining 10% is returned to the circulation via the lymphatic system, preventing net fluid accumulation (edema) under normal conditions. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"What are the major functions of the lymphatic system described in this lecture?","choices":["An accessory route for fluid/protein return to blood (preventing edema), a major route for GI nutrient absorption, and a role in immune function","Regulating heart rate directly through parasympathetic fibers that run alongside the major lymphatic trunks","Only removing metabolic waste products, playing no role whatsoever in immunity, edema prevention, or nutrient absorption","Producing red blood cells exclusively within specialized lymphoid tissue found throughout the mesentery"],"answer":0,"exp":"The lymphatic system serves as an accessory route for fluid and protein to flow from interstitial spaces back to the blood (important in preventing edema), is a major route for absorption of nutrients from the GI tract, and plays an important role in the immune system. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"What structural feature of lymphatic capillaries allows easy entry of high-molecular-weight substances and proteins while preventing backflow?","choices":["A continuous basement membrane with no gaps, restricting entry to only very small lipid-soluble molecules","Overlapping endothelial cells that form valves preventing backflow while allowing easy entry of large molecules/proteins","Lymphatic capillaries have no valve-like structures at all, relying entirely on external muscle compression for flow","Tight junctions with no overlap between cells, preventing entry of any protein larger than albumin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Overlap between endothelial cells in lymphatic capillaries forms flap-like valves that prevent backflow of lymph while allowing easy entry of high-molecular-weight substances and proteins that cannot easily enter blood capillaries. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"What two major factors determine the rate of lymph flow?","choices":["Lymph flow is constant and unregulated, remaining completely independent of both interstitial pressure and any muscular pumping activity","Only blood pressure in the aorta, which is transmitted passively downstream through the entire lymphatic vessel network","Only the activity of the lymphatic pump itself, since interstitial fluid pressure has no measurable influence on flow rate","Interstitial fluid (IF) pressure and the activity of the lymphatic pump (smooth muscle contraction, external compression, arterial pulsation, and valves)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Lymph flow is a function of interstitial fluid (IF) pressure and the activity of the lymphatic pump — driven by smooth muscle contraction in lymph vessel walls, external compression (e.g., skeletal muscle), pulsation of surrounding arteries, and one-way lymphatic valves. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"What determines how much blood flow a given tissue receives under local control?","choices":["Each tissue controls its own blood flow in proportion to its needs (oxygen delivery, nutrient delivery, waste removal), closely related to metabolic rate","Blood flow is fixed and unrelated to tissue activity, determined entirely by vessel anatomy that was established during fetal development","Blood flow is determined solely by total circulating blood volume, entirely independent of local tissue metabolic demand or activity level","Only the autonomic nervous system determines local tissue blood flow, with no contribution from local metabolic factors whatsoever"],"answer":0,"exp":"Each tissue controls its own blood flow in proportion to its needs — delivery of oxygen and nutrients, removal of CO2 and other metabolites, and transport of hormones — with flow closely related to the tissue's metabolic rate. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"What percentage of the heart's energy is normally derived from fatty acids at rest, and what happens metabolically during ischemia?","choices":["Ischemia dramatically increases fatty acid oxidation exclusively, sparing the glucose reserves entirely and producing no lactic acid buildup at all","The heart relies almost entirely on circulating ketone bodies for energy at rest, switching to fatty acids only during ischemic stress","About 100% of resting cardiac energy comes from glucose oxidation; ischemia then causes a shift toward exclusive fatty acid metabolism","About 70% from fatty acids at rest; ischemia forces reliance on anaerobic glycolysis, consuming glucose and producing lactic acid (a likely cause of cardiac pain)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Under resting conditions, cardiac muscle derives about 70% of its energy from fatty acids rather than carbohydrates; under anaerobic/ischemic conditions, the heart must rely on anaerobic glycolysis, consuming large quantities of glucose and producing lactic acid — likely a cause of cardiac pain during ischemia. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What determines the degree of damage from coronary occlusion, and how quickly can collateral flow develop after a sudden occlusion?","choices":["Collateral circulation matters only in slowly progressive atherosclerosis; sudden occlusions always cause complete and irreversible infarction regardless of any anastomoses","Collateral vessels typically take several years to develop meaningful flow, so acute coronary occlusions cause maximal, unmodifiable damage regardless of anastomoses","Damage is determined largely by the degree of collateral circulation; small anastomoses dilate within seconds of sudden occlusion, and collateral flow can reach near-normal levels within about a month","Damage is determined solely by the diameter of the vessel that occludes; collateral anastomoses essentially do not exist within the coronary circulation"],"answer":2,"exp":"The degree of heart muscle damage from coronary occlusion is determined largely by the degree of collateral circulation; small anastomoses dilate within seconds of a sudden occlusion (though initially providing less than half the needed flow), and collateral flow often increases to near-normal levels within about a month, aiding recovery. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What causes the first heart sound, and how does its pitch/loudness compare to the second heart sound?","choices":["A-V valve closure plus backflow of blood against the ventricular wall; it is louder and lower-pitched than the second heart sound","Reverberation of blood within the ventricles during rapid filling; it is softer and higher-pitched than the second heart sound","Semilunar valve closure alone, with no contribution from blood backflow; it is identical in pitch to the second heart sound","Atrial contraction against closed A-V valves; it is typically inaudible without amplification equipment"],"answer":0,"exp":"The first heart sound (S1) is caused by A-V valve closure plus backflow of blood against the ventricular wall; it is louder and lower-pitched than the second heart sound (S2), which is caused by aortic/pulmonic valve closure and is shorter and higher-pitched. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What causes the third and fourth heart sounds, and when are they considered normal versus pathological?","choices":["Both sounds are produced exclusively by semilunar valve closure occurring earlier than normal, and are always considered entirely normal findings at any age, including in elderly hypertensive patients","S3 is generated by mitral valve closure and S4 is generated by tricuspid valve closure, with both occurring simultaneously alongside the first heart sound in every healthy adult","S3 and S4 are both caused by turbulent flow through stenotic semilunar valves during systole, exactly like the mechanism that produces the first and second heart sounds in every patient","S3 is caused by reverberation of blood in the ventricles (middle third of diastole); S4 is caused by atrial contraction late in diastole — both may be physiological in young children/thin people but are otherwise usually pathological"],"answer":3,"exp":"The third heart sound (S3) is caused by reverberation of blood in the ventricles during the middle third of diastole; the fourth heart sound (S4) is caused by atrial contraction (\"atrial kick\") late in diastole. Both are low pitched and can be physiologically audible in young children or thin people, but are otherwise usually pathological findings. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is the general function of the arterial baroreceptor reflex, and where are the key baroreceptors located?","choices":["Baroreceptors are located exclusively in the pulmonary veins and atrial walls, unrelated to systemic pressure","It is important only for regulating respiratory rate, with stretch receptors located in the bronchial walls","It is important for long-term pressure regulation only; baroreceptors are chemosensitive cells located in the renal afferent arterioles","It is important for short-term regulation of arterial pressure; baroreceptors (stretch receptors) are located in the carotid sinus and aortic arch"],"answer":3,"exp":"The arterial baroreceptor reflex is important in the short-term regulation of arterial pressure; it is initiated by stretch receptors (baroreceptors/pressoreceptors) located in the walls of the carotid sinus (at the carotid bifurcation) and the aortic arch. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"By what nerves do signals from the carotid sinus and aortic arch baroreceptors reach the nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS)?","choices":["Both the carotid sinus and aortic arch signals travel exclusively via the accessory nerve to the medulla","Both pathways terminate in the cerebellum instead of reaching the nucleus tractus solitarius","Carotid sinus signals travel via Hering's nerve to the glossopharyngeal nerve; aortic arch signals travel via the vagus nerve","Both carotid sinus and aortic arch signals travel exclusively through the hypoglossal nerve"],"answer":2,"exp":"Signals from the carotid sinus baroreceptors travel via Hering's nerve to the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) and then to the nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS) of the medulla; signals from the aortic arch travel through the vagus nerve (CN X) to the NTS. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Over what pressure range do carotid sinus baroreceptors respond, and at what pressure is the reflex most sensitive?","choices":["200-300 mm Hg, most sensitive at 250 mm Hg","Baroreceptors respond equally at all pressures with no peak sensitivity","60-180 mm Hg, most sensitive around 100 mm Hg","0-50 mm Hg, most sensitive at 25 mm Hg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Carotid sinus baroreceptors respond to pressures between about 60 and 180 mm Hg, with the reflex being most sensitive around a pressure of 100 mm Hg — near the normal mean arterial pressure. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"As arterial pressure rises and carotid sinus impulses increase, what two effects does this produce?","choices":["Only activation of the sympathetic vasoconstrictor system and adrenal catecholamine release, with no change in vagal tone","A transient increase in both vasoconstrictor and vagal activity that cancels out, leaving pressure unchanged","Activation of the vasoconstrictor center and inhibition of the vagal center — both working to raise pressure further","Inhibition of the vasoconstrictor center and activation of the vagal center — both working to lower pressure back toward normal"],"answer":3,"exp":"As arterial pressure increases and carotid sinus baroreceptor impulses increase, this causes (1) inhibition of the vasoconstrictor center and (2) activation of the vagal center — both actions work together to lower heart rate and vascular resistance, returning pressure toward normal. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What happens to baroreceptor sensitivity when they are exposed to a sustained change in blood pressure?","choices":["Baroreceptor firing rate increases progressively over days of sustained hypertension, amplifying their long-term corrective effect","Baroreceptors \"reset\" and attenuate their potency in response to sustained pressure changes, which is why they are considered unimportant for long-term pressure control","Baroreceptors maintain a constant sensitivity indefinitely, which is why they dominate both the short-term and the long-term pressure regulation","Resetting occurs only with sudden pressure spikes and reverses completely within minutes, restoring full baseline sensitivity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Baroreceptors \"reset\" when exposed to sustained increases or decreases in blood pressure, attenuating their potency over time — this adaptation is why baroreceptors are considered unimportant for long-term arterial pressure control, even though they effectively buffer short-term/daily variations. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What stimulates the carotid and aortic chemoreceptors, and at what pressure threshold do they typically become activated?","choices":["They are stimulated by rising blood glucose levels and are activated continuously regardless of the arterial pressure","They respond only to changes in plasma osmolarity and are located exclusively within the hypothalamus","They are stimulated by oxygen excess and CO2 depletion, and are activated only above 150 mm Hg","They are stimulated by oxygen lack, CO2 excess, or H+ excess, and are typically not stimulated until pressure falls below 80 mm Hg"],"answer":3,"exp":"Carotid and aortic chemoreceptors are chemosensitive cells stimulated by oxygen lack, CO2 excess, or H+ ion excess; located in the carotid bodies and aortic arch, they are not stimulated until arterial pressure falls below about 80 mm Hg, at which point their activation excites the vasomotor center. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What triggers the CNS ischemic response, and how powerful is it compared to other pressure control mechanisms?","choices":["It is a weak, minor reflex triggered only by mild changes in cerebrospinal fluid pH, producing negligible and clinically insignificant effects on systemic arterial pressure","It is triggered by an excessively high cerebral blood flow that washes out CO2, which then inhibits the vasomotor centre and produces a modest, gradual decrease in the arterial pressure","It has no relationship to CO2 levels at all, and is instead triggered entirely by falling cerebral oxygen tension acting directly on the carotid body chemoreceptors","It is triggered by reduced cerebral blood flow causing CO2 buildup, which stimulates the vasomotor center to increase arterial pressure; it is one of the most powerful activators of the sympathetic vasoconstrictor system"],"answer":3,"exp":"The CNS ischemic response is activated by cerebral ischemia: reduced cerebral blood flow causes local CO2 buildup, which stimulates the vasomotor center and dramatically increases arterial pressure — this response is one of the most powerful activators of the sympathetic vasoconstrictor system, serving as an emergency mechanism to preserve brain perfusion. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"How do low-pressure receptors in the atria and pulmonary arteries respond to increased blood volume?","choices":["They minimize arterial pressure changes by lowering arterial pressure in response to increased blood volume, partly by enhancing Na/water excretion (decreased ADH, increased GFR, decreased Na reabsorption)","They only respond to arterial baroreceptor input relayed secondarily from the carotid sinus and aortic arch, with no independent sensitivity to atrial stretch","They amplify arterial pressure changes in response to increased blood volume, partly by suppressing Na and water excretion (increased ADH, decreased GFR)","They have no functional response to blood volume changes at all, since ADH secretion and the glomerular filtration rate are both controlled solely by the renin-angiotensin system"],"answer":0,"exp":"Low-pressure receptors in the atria and pulmonary arteries minimize arterial pressure changes in response to blood volume changes; increases in blood volume activate these receptors, which lower arterial pressure and enhance sodium/water excretion by decreasing ADH secretion, increasing glomerular filtration rate, and decreasing renal sodium reabsorption. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What happens to nerve fibers from the nasal versus temporal retina at the optic chiasm?","choices":["All of the fibres from both the nasal and the temporal retina cross completely over to the opposite side, leaving essentially no uncrossed fibres whatsoever","No fibers cross at the optic chiasm at all; each resulting optic tract instead carries signals from only a single entire eye's retina","Only fibers from the temporal retina cross over to the opposite side, joining the nasal fibers of that same eye to form each optic tract","Fibers from the nasal half of each retina cross to the opposite side and join fibers from the opposite eye's temporal retina to form the optic tracts; temporal fibers do not cross"],"answer":3,"exp":"At the optic chiasm, fibers from the nasal halves of each retina cross to the opposite side, joining uncrossed fibers from the opposite eye's temporal retina to form the optic tracts — allowing each hemisphere to process the corresponding half of the visual field from both eyes. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What are the two principal functions of the dorsal lateral geniculate nucleus in the visual pathway?","choices":["Only relaying information onward to the primary visual cortex, with those pathways bypassing the optic radiation entirely","Regenerating rhodopsin for the rods and cones of the retina via a direct feedback loop with the choroid","Directly generating the full conscious visual perception before any signal even reaches the occipital cortex","Relaying information to the primary visual cortex via the optic radiation, and \"gate control\" of information passing to the visual cortex"],"answer":3,"exp":"The dorsal lateral geniculate nucleus (LGN) of the thalamus has two principal functions: relaying visual information to the primary visual cortex via the optic radiation, and \"gate control\" — regulating how much information passes through to the cortex. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"How are signals from the two eyes organized within the LGN's layered structure?","choices":["Signals from both of the eyes are completely mixed together within every one of the LGN's six distinct cellular layers simultaneously","All six layers of the LGN receive input exclusively from the ipsilateral eye, with no contralateral input reaching it at all","Only signals from the contralateral eye ever reach the LGN, since all ipsilateral fibers terminate earlier at the optic chiasm","Signals from the two eyes are kept separate — layers 2, 3, and 5 receive input from the ipsilateral eye, while layers 1, 4, and 6 receive input from the contralateral eye"],"answer":3,"exp":"In the LGN, signals from the two eyes are kept segregated by layer: layers 2, 3, and 5 receive input from the ipsilateral eye, while layers 1, 4, and 6 receive input from the contralateral eye — separation that is lost once signals reach the primary visual cortex. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Where is the primary visual cortex located, and how is the fovea represented there compared to peripheral retina?","choices":["In the parietal lobe near the postcentral gyrus, with the peripheral retina occupying nearly all of the available cortical surface area","The primary visual cortex has essentially no topographic organisation at all, with the fovea and the periphery randomly intermixed throughout the whole tissue","In the temporal lobe near the hippocampus, with the peripheral retina given several hundred times more representation than the fovea itself","In the calcarine fissure of the occipital lobe, with the fovea (part of the macula) having several hundred times more cortical representation than an equivalent area of peripheral retina"],"answer":3,"exp":"The primary visual cortex lies in the calcarine fissure of the occipital lobe; the macula (which includes the fovea) has several hundred times more cortical representation than an equivalent area of peripheral retina, reflecting its role in high-acuity central vision. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"How are signals from the two eyes organized within the primary visual cortex, in contrast to the LGN?","choices":["Only one eye's signal ever reaches the visual cortex, since the other eye's fibers terminate entirely within the superior colliculus","Signals from the two eyes are averaged together directly within the LGN itself, arriving at the cortex as a single, already-fused image","Separation of signals from the two eyes is lost — signals from one eye enter every other cortical column, alternating with the other eye, allowing the cortex to compare the two images","They remain completely segregated by cortical column exactly as they already were in the LGN, with no mixing of any kind occurring anywhere within the cortex"],"answer":2,"exp":"Unlike the LGN, where signals from the two eyes remain segregated by layer, this separation is lost in the primary visual cortex — signals from one eye enter every other cortical column, alternating with signals from the other eye, allowing the cortex to determine whether the two eyes' signals match (important for stereopsis). — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What three types of information are thought to be processed by parallel pathways in visual perception?","choices":["Taste, smell, and touch","Motion, depth/form, and color","Only pain and temperature, unrelated to vision","Only color information, with no separate pathways for motion or form"],"answer":1,"exp":"Visual perception is thought to be mediated by three parallel processing pathways handling motion, depth and form, and color — though exactly how the brain integrates these into a unified conscious visual perception is not well understood. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What is the primary visual cortex mainly concerned with analyzing in the visual scene?","choices":["Absolute color values only, with dedicated neurons for each specific wavelength but no attention to contrast borders","Only the overall brightness of the entire visual field, averaged across the retina with no spatial detail preserved","Contrasts in the visual scene, including the sharpness and orientation of contrast borders — with specific neurons stimulated by specific line orientations","Sound localisation cues relayed directly from the inferior colliculus, integrated together with retinal signal timing information"],"answer":2,"exp":"The visual signal reaching the primary visual cortex is concerned mainly with contrasts in the visual scene; sharper contrast produces greater stimulation, and the cortex also detects the direction/orientation of each line and border, with specific neurons responding to specific line orientations. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which subcortical structure receives retinal input responsible for the pupillary light reflex and lens accommodation?","choices":["The pretectal nuclei","The superior colliculus","The suprachiasmatic nucleus","The ventral lateral geniculate nucleus"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pretectal nuclei receive retinal projections responsible for the pupillary light reflex and accommodation of the lens, distinct from the suprachiasmatic nucleus (circadian rhythm control) and superior colliculus (rapid eye movement control). — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which subcortical structure receives retinal input thought to control circadian rhythms?","choices":["The superior colliculus within the midbrain","The primary visual cortex directly","The suprachiasmatic nucleus of the hypothalamus","The pretectal nuclei of the midbrain"],"answer":2,"exp":"The suprachiasmatic nucleus of the hypothalamus receives direct retinal projections thought to be involved in the control of circadian rhythms. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What retinal-projection-related function does the superior colliculus serve?","choices":["Regulation of pupillary constriction alone","Slow production of aqueous humor","Rapid directional movement of both eyes","Long-term control of circadian rhythms"],"answer":2,"exp":"The superior colliculus receives retinal projections and is involved in the rapid directional movement of both eyes, and also contributes to the negative-feedback mechanism that keeps objects of attention fixed on the fovea. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which pair of extraocular muscles is responsible for rotating the eyes, as opposed to moving them side to side or up and down?","choices":["None of the extraocular muscles rotate the eye","Medial and lateral recti","Superior and inferior obliques","Superior and inferior recti"],"answer":2,"exp":"The superior and inferior oblique muscles rotate the eyes, while the medial and lateral recti move the eyes side to side, and the superior and inferior recti move the eyes up and down. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What controls voluntary fixation movements of the eyes, versus the involuntary fixation mechanism?","choices":["Voluntary fixation requires no cortical involvement of any kind whatsoever, arising entirely as a brainstem reflex that is generated by the pretectal nuclei acting directly through the superior colliculus","Both voluntary and involuntary fixation are controlled by the exact same area of premotor cortex, with no separate involvement from the occipital lobe or superior colliculus at all","Involuntary fixation is controlled by the cerebellum exclusively, coordinating eye position purely through direct connections to the vestibular nuclei alone, with no cortical input","Voluntary fixation is controlled by an area in the premotor cortex; involuntary fixation (\"locking on\" to an object) is controlled by secondary visual areas of the occipital cortex, via negative feedback at the level of the superior colliculus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Voluntary fixation movements are controlled by an area in the premotor cortex, while the involuntary fixation mechanism (which \"locks\" the eyes onto an object of attention found via voluntary fixation) is controlled by secondary visual areas of the occipital cortex, using negative feedback at the level of the superior colliculus to keep the object of attention on the fovea. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 45"}]};
